// Reordering with external actions, under the Java Memory Model.
//
// Source: Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008), §5.3.5. The thesis' program:
//
//              r1=y                         r2=x
//              if (r1==1)                   y=r2
//                 x=1
//              else {print "!"; x=1}
//
// The JMM FORBIDS r1 = r2 = 1: r2 = 1 needs the race on x committed, and the
// rule for external actions commits the print with it; r1 = 1 then needs the
// race on y, after which the print cannot be performed. Swapping print "!" and
// x=1 in the else-branch lifts that rule, and r1 = r2 = 1 becomes allowed, so
// the JMM invalidates reordering with external actions. Under the DRF
// guarantee the reordering is safe (Table 1.1).
//
// MoRDor has no external actions, so the print is a comment and the program
// below is the one without it: x := 1 on both branches. That is the shape of
// Figure 6.1, which sMRD allows by lifting the write over the branch, so the
// sMRD fallback answers a different program from the one the verdict is about.
// litmus-tests/sevcik_thesis/Skip/Reordering with external actions.lit is
// MoRDor's own variant.
//
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  r1 := y;
  if (r1 = 1) {
    x := 1;
  } else {
    // print "!"
    x := 1;
  }
} ||| {
  r2 := x;
  y := r2
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 1) [JMM] "cannot result in r1 = r2 = 1 in the JMM"
